{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfREb2YCKkBr7y4RSDRb9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RunJTS5WcJY6oXJNnhP2DC","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"find . -iname \"CLAUDE.md\" -not -path \"./node_modules/*\"; echo ---; cat README.md | head -100"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3982,"cache_read_input_tokens":33678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3982},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0dd7ed4f-100c-4188-9a34-077aa0b6aa25","uuid":"418fb11e-a860-4afd-b747-e68b0a353d1e","timestamp":"2026-09-26T02:07:56.764Z","request_id":"req_011CfREb2CbxnrHh93ZFBhnm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RunJTS5WcJY6oXJNnhP2DC":{"command":"cd /Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-219 && find . -iname \"CLAUDE.md\" -not -path \"./node_modules/*\"; echo ---; cat README.md | head -100"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01RunJTS5WcJY6oXJNnhP2DC":{"cwd":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-219"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RunJTS5WcJY6oXJNnhP2DC","type":"tool_result","content":"---\n# Attavo\n\nSoftware teams are about to run more agents than engineers, and every one of those agents will open pull requests faster than a person can read them. The bottleneck of the next decade is not writing code; it is knowing which code to trust. Attavo makes that knowable. It turns a specification into executable acceptance criteria, binds a human approval to a commit, runs the build and the judgment as separate invocations with separate objectives, records every intervention with a class, and merges only what carries an attestation. The operator does three things: approve the spec, rule on the escalations that are theirs alone, and merge. Everything else is the product's, and everything the product does is on the record.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`bin/attavo doctor <tenant-root>` is the definition of done. It asserts three proofs and exits non-zero until all three hold:\n\n1. Every gate named in the tenant's manifest rejects the fixture it must reject.\n2. One work item has traversed every stage — spec, approval, build, verify — all four introduced by one merge commit.\n3. No commit on the tenant's default branch lacks a record.\n\n`tests/test_doctor.py` holds the three proofs as tests. They fail against this repository, which has no manifest, and that is expected: this repository is the product, not a tenant. A phase is done when its proof holds, never when its work is finished.\n\n## The spec contract (Phase 1)\n\nA spec is one artifact with a line in it. Above the line — the problem and the claims — is amendable after approval at the cost of one act. Below the line — the criteria, which execute — is the only thing approval freezes.\n\n- `attavo spec-gate N` runs a work item's criteria against the tree, then against the tree plus its stub overlay, and refuses by name: wrong headings, an empty claim, a criterion that is prose, a criterion that already passes, or one that still fails with the stub applied. A stub may carry `stub/.removed`, one path per line, deleted from the stubbed copy after the overlay and before the criteria run — the overlay's one way to express a deletion, not just an add or a replace — and the gate refuses by name, before any criterion runs, an entry naming a path absent from the tree, a path the stub also supplies, or a path outside the tree. Once `attavo approval-status` would print `valid` at `HEAD`, the gate runs no criterion at all: it writes `status: approved`, naming the spec and the approval it rests on, and exits 0 — the criteria after approval are build's and verify's to run and carry into the attestation, not the gate's to re-check. A third verdict: when the approval is no longer valid but a build already ran after it (an amendment above the line expired the approval), the gate runs each criterion against the tree only — no stub — and expects it to pass; `status: built`, criteria entries carrying `on_tree` only (`on_stub: null`); refusal names the first criterion still failing on the tree. Every run writes the next `spec-NN.json`.\n- `attavo approve N --by LOGIN` binds approval to the commit that produced the latest `spec-NN.json` with status `pass` or `built`, and refuses a `LOGIN` not in the manifest's `[operators].approvers`.\n- `attavo approval-status N` reports `valid`, `expired`, or `absent` — expired the moment the spec directory moves, above the line or below it alike.\n\nAll three run from the tenant root; `N` is read from the branch (`attavo/N-*`) when omitted. The full contract is in the Phase 1 issue.\n\n## Build (Phase 2)\n\n`attavo build N` refuses (exit 1, naming the cause, no record) unless the manifest declares `[executor].build` and `[tests].select` and `[tests].run`, and unless `attavo approval-status` would print `valid`. It invokes the named adapter — `adapters/<name>/run`, resolved first under the tenant's own `attavo/adapters/`, then under this product's `adapters/` — with the spec, the corpus, and the branch. After each return it checks that every path the adapter touched matches a spec claim, then runs the criteria against the tree, the tests `[tests].select` and `[tests].run` name for the touched paths, and — when the manifest declares `[checks].command` — the tenant's own whole-tree check, from the tenant root. `pass` requires every criterion, the test run, and the tenant's checks (if declared) to exit 0; a non-zero exit anywhere iterates, with the failure as feedback, up to a fixed budget. `[checks]` is optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused, unlike `[tests]`. Before the adapter is invoked, `attavo build` asks whether this machine can run the checks at all: of `[checks].probe`, a cheap command run once inside `[checks].probe_timeout_s` (required with it; exit 0 means yes, anything else or the bound is an environment refusal), or, for a tenant that declares no probe, by running `[checks].command` whole (exit 2 means no). The feed says \"checking this machine can run the checks\" before and the answer after. `[checks].command` is what is fast and decisive at the gate; what the forge runs as its own required check need not be run twice. The adapter is given one test command, `attavo test N` (`ATTAVO_TEST_COMMAND`, with `ATTAVO_TEST_TIMEOUT_S` when the tenant declares `[tests].call_timeout_s`), not the checks: it selects, through `[tests].select`, for every path the item has changed since it left the default branch, committed or not (the records directory aside), runs them through `[tests].run`, exits as the run does, and at the bound ends the run with everything it started and exits 124. A selector that maps a path it does not know to nothing hides the tests that would have caught the change; a tenant's selector should send such a path to the whole suite. A `question` from the adapter halts the loop at once (exit 3); nothing answers it in Phase 2. Every run writes the next `build-NN.json`, with the adapter's transcript beside it as `build-NN.log`. Nothing dispatches build; it is typed by the operator. The full contract is in the Phase 2 issue.\n\n## Layout\n\n- `bin/attavo` — the command: `doctor`, `spec-gate`, `approve`, `approval-status`, `build`.\n- `schema/manifest.schema.json` — the shape of a tenant's `attavo.toml`.\n- `schema/records/` — the shape of each stage's record.\n- `adapters/` — the adapter contract (`adapters/README.md`) and two adapters: `claude-code` and `fixture`.\n- `action.yml` — pins Attavo at a tag and puts `attavo` on `PATH`.\n- `templates/workflows/` — three workflows a tenant copies into its own repository (spec-gate, approve, coherence); nothing here is vendored or run by this one. The coherence job mints and signs the attestation on the merge group, against the commit that lands — no attestation record is ever written to the tree; the signed statement lives in the forge's attestation store.\n- `tests/` — the doctor's proofs and the spec and build contracts' rejection tests, with their fixtures under `tests/fixtures/`.\n\nThis repository contains no tenant name, path, or command string.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0dd7ed4f-100c-4188-9a34-077aa0b6aa25","uuid":"dee1126e-e771-479a-8613-efdcaef4dd1e","timestamp":"2026-09-26T02:07:56.883Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\n# Attavo\n\nSoftware teams are about to run more agents than engineers, and every one of those agents will open pull requests faster than a person can read them. The bottleneck of the next decade is not writing code; it is knowing which code to trust. Attavo makes that knowable. It turns a specification into executable acceptance criteria, binds a human approval to a commit, runs the build and the judgment as separate invocations with separate objectives, records every intervention with a class, and merges only what carries an attestation. The operator does three things: approve the spec, rule on the escalatio